On Sat, 10 Oct 2026 11:57:53 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> I use notmuch | to pipe the contents into a command, specifically `git
> am`. This works great when I'm on the default@ workspace which has a
> colocated git repo. However when I'm on the mail@ workspace it fails
> because there is no git repo.

I've posted a command for this as "[PATCH] mail: Add am.sh to apply
patches in any workspace" [1]. It works like git am but writes to the
jj workspace you are in, so it works in mail@ as well as default@:

    | cd ~/lab/mail.jj && ~/lab/default.jj/libexec/mail/am.sh

Leave out the cd if notmuch already runs the command in mail.jj. git
still does the mail parsing and runs git apply, and neither of those
needs a repo. jj then records each patch as its own change, with the
patch's author, date, and message. Each patch goes into @, which must
be empty, and it starts a new change on top, the way git am leaves
HEAD clean. You can pipe a single message or a whole series thread.
It skips cover letters and replies.

> Ideally I'd find a command that I can run in | that does a git am to the
> current directory, but using the .git dir from default@ workspace, if
> that's possible.

It is possible, but it does the wrong thing, so I didn't build on it.
I tried it in a scratch repo:

    git --git-dir=~/lab/default.jj/.git --work-tree=. am

git am commits on top of that repo's HEAD, which is default@'s parent
on trunk, using default@'s index. That makes the new commits trunk's
tree plus the patch, not mail's. They also miss any patched file that
trunk lacks. Then the next jj command in default.jj prints "Reset the
working copy parent to the new Git HEAD" and moves default@ onto those
commits. The patch also lands in mail.jj's files, but nothing records
it there.

A colocated workspace for mail.jj would avoid the whole problem. jj
0.45 can't make a second workspace colocated, as far as I know, so
am.sh goes the other way and drops the need for a repo.

